      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To quantify the number of asthma attacks treated in the emergency room of a public hospital and to study seasonal fluctuations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "A retrospective survey was conducted in the pediatrician emergency room of General Hospital Agustin O'Horan in Merida, Yucatan, Mexico during one year (January 1 to December 31st, 2005). A total of 7,674 emergency room consultations were registered during a period of one year. All patients were younger than 14 years old."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the conditions treated, acute asthma (10.3%) was the third most common diagnosis. There were 622 children with asthma, 376 boys (60%). Most of the asthma consultations (22.6%) involved children of 1 year old of age. The consultation for asthma attacks increased in December (18.4%), October (14.1%) and September (12.2%). A significative association was found between the monthly number of emergency room visits due to acute asthma and upper respiratory infections (r = 0.9059, p = < 0.001, I.C. 99%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The acute asthma is one of the most frequent emergency consult causes in children and there was a positive association between acute asthma and upper respiratory infections."
        }
      ]},
